<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.14.1: Using Point-Slope Form to Write the Equation of a Line</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00305</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.14.1: Using Point-Slope Form to Write the Equation of a Line</md:title>
    <md:uuid>e795ae3c-1223-42fc-aef7-4d4ab02cfe7c</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up Activity</title>
<para>Your teacher will assign you one pair of the points shown.</para>
<para>Points for Line A: \( (-1, 5) \) and \( (1, 1) \)Points for Line B: \( (-1, -1) \) and \( (1, -5) \)</para>
<para>Use your assigned points to find the following:</para>

<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
  Find the slope of the line.</para>
</problem><solution>
  Compare your answer:
    <para>Line A: slope: \( m= \frac{1-5}{1-(-1)}=-2 \)Line B: slope: \( m= \frac{-5-(-1)}{1-(-1)}=-2 \)</para>
    
    <para>Remember to use the slope formula to find the slope: \( m=\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1} \).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>


<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
  Write the equation of the line in slope-intercept form.</para>
</problem><solution>
  
    <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Line A: equation \( y = -2x + 3 \)Line B: equation \( y = -2x - 3 \)</para>
    
    <para>Remember to use the point-slope form for the equation of the line to write the equation: \( y - y_1 = m_1(x - x_1) \). Write the equation in slope-intercept form \( y = mx + b \), where the slope is \( m \) and the \( y \)-intercept is \( b \).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>


<exercise>
  <problem>
  <para>
  Compare your equation to your partner’s equation. What were some things that were the same? What were some things that were different</para>
</problem><solution>
  
    <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here are some samples.</para>
    
    <list>
      <item>Both lines have a negative slope.</item>
      <item>Both lines have the same slope.</item>
      <item>The \( y \)-intercepts are different.</item>
      <item>The \( y \)-intercepts have opposite signs.</item>
    </list>
  
  </solution></exercise></section>
</content>
